- 'Why keep it simple?','A compact, low-parts-count control network fits this small amplifier. That is a plausible design benefit, not a documented statement of Fender’s intent.',
- 'Capacitor values are read from Fender’s drawing; frequency response and exact knob tapers have not been measured.'),
- card('inputs','Inputs & channel bridging','top','Bridging adds another voice before the shared controls.',
- 'A patch cable can feed the guitar into both channels. That gives you two first-stage signals to blend with the interacting volume controls, rather than turning the amp into two separate power sections.',
+ 'Try a Tone sweep at two different Volume settings, then move the unused channel’s Volume and try again. The same number on the Tone knob won’t necessarily give you the same balance.',
+ 'This isn’t the later Fender bass-and-treble tone stack. The drawing shows a 500 pF cap and a 5 nF cap routing highs around and through parts of the volume network. The 500 pF one ties Tone to the Instrument Volume next to it, so that’s the channel with the bright cap.',
+ 'With the Instrument Volume at zero','Robinette works through this case: with the Instrument Volume all the way down, the bright cap pulls the top of the tone circuit toward ground and the Tone knob loses its brightest settings.',
+ 'Cap values are read from Fender’s drawing. Frequency response and pot tapers haven’t been measured.'),
+ card('inputs','Inputs & channel bridging','top','Jumper the channels and you’re blending two first stages.',
+ 'Run a short patch cable from one channel’s spare input to the other channel and your guitar feeds both halves of the 12AY7. Now both Volumes are live and you set them against each other.',
  [['First tube','One half of a 12AY7 per channel'],['Input network','Switched jacks · 68 kΩ resistors']],
- 'Compare one channel with a bridged setup at a similar listening level. The extra channel changes the blend and loading; louder alone is not proof of a richer sound.',
- 'The two input triodes have the same basic gain-stage arrangement and share a cathode resistor. Both arrive at the common volume/tone network. Jack switching changes the input network when more than one plug is inserted.',
- 'The signal still meets a tube first','The Volume controls sit after the first stages. Turning them down does not reduce what a hot pickup or boost sends into those stages in the same way as reducing the incoming signal.',
- 'The drawing supports the circuit relationship. No particular patch-cable arrangement or level increase is promised.'),
- card('preamp','12AY7 input stage','rear','The original input tube is part of the gain recipe.',
- 'Fender specifies a 12AY7 at the input. A 12AX7 in that socket changes the gain and operating behavior, so “it fits” does not mean the amp keeps the same response.',
+ 'It’s a well-known tweed habit, and there’s no agreed best setting. Compare jumpered against one channel at about the same loudness, because louder on its own will fool you.',
+ 'Each channel has two switched input jacks with 68 kΩ resistors, and the two input triodes share a cathode resistor. Both feed the common volume and tone network. The jumper itself is a player trick. It isn’t on Fender’s drawing.',
+ 'The first tube still gets hit first','The Volumes come after the first stages. Turning them down doesn’t reduce what a hot pickup or a boost sends into those stages. Turning down the guitar or the pedal does.',
+ 'The circuit relationship is from the drawing. No particular patch arrangement or level change is promised.'),
+ card('preamp','12AY7 input stage','rear','It’s a 12AY7 up front, not a 12AX7.',
+ 'Fender’s drawing puts a 12AY7 in the first socket. A 12AX7 fits and has more gain, so the amp doesn’t respond the same way with one in there.',
  [['V1','12AY7 · two input triodes'],['Shared cathode','820 Ω · 25 µF bypass']],
- 'When comparing two 5E3s, check the first tube before blaming the speaker or transformers for a difference in how soon the amp gets lively.',
- 'A 12AY7 generally gives less voltage gain than a 12AX7 in this sort of stage. The following stages and controls still decide how that translates into headroom and drive; the tube name alone does not identify where distortion begins.',
- 'The channels share more than a cabinet','Both input halves share the cathode resistor and bypass capacitor. They are separate input paths inside a connected circuit, not two wholly independent preamps.',
- 'The small-tube positions follow the drawing’s rear socket order. Spacing and glass shapes are illustrative.',true),
- card('phase-inverter','12AX7 phase inverter','rear','One tube half gives the pair opposite jobs.',
- 'The 5E3 uses a split-load, or cathodyne, phase inverter. It takes opposite-polarity signals from one tube half to drive the two 6V6s.',
+ 'Owners do try a 12AX7 here, and it can make the amp break up earlier. Whether that’s better is a matter of taste. If two 5E3s get lively at different points, check the first tube before you blame the speaker or the transformers.',
+ 'With less gain in the first stage, more of the overdrive tends to come from the later stages and the power tubes. The tube type alone doesn’t tell you where the distortion starts. The stages and controls after it decide that.',
+ 'The two channels share a cathode','Both halves of the 12AY7 sit on one 820 Ω cathode resistor with a 25 µF bypass. So the two inputs are separate paths inside a connected circuit, not two independent preamps.',
+ 'Small-tube positions follow the socket order on Fender’s layout. Spacing and glass shapes are illustrative.',true),
+ card('phase-inverter','12AX7 phase inverter','rear','Half a 12AX7 drives both power tubes.',
+ 'The 5E3 uses a split-load phase inverter, also called a cathodyne. One half of the 12AX7 puts out two opposite-polarity signals, one for each 6V6.',
  [['V2','12AX7 · gain stage plus phase inverter'],['Split-load network','56 kΩ plate and cathode loads']],
- 'The inverter’s available drive and behavior when pushed are part of the amp’s overdrive character. “Two 6V6s” does not make it behave like a later Deluxe Reverb.',
- 'The other half of V2 supplies voltage gain first. The cathodyne then splits the drive rather than providing another large voltage-gain stage. Its circuit and the load of the output tubes matter together.',
- 'Avoid a single-part explanation','Hard-playing distortion may involve the input stage, following gain stage, inverter, output pair and speaker. No clipping waveform was measured for this model.',
- 'The drawing establishes the topology. The marker identifies V2, not a separately visible phase-inverter component.',true),
- card('power-tubes','Cathode-biased 6V6 pair','rear','The same tube family, a different kind of Deluxe.',
- 'The two 6V6s share a cathode-bias network. The 5E3 drawing also has no global feedback loop returning the speaker output to an earlier stage—two useful differences from the blackface Deluxe Reverb.',
+ 'Some analysts and owners credit part of the tweed Deluxe’s rough edge to this stage, on top of the 6V6s. It’s one of the reasons people give for a tweed and a blackface Deluxe sounding so different when both run two 6V6s.',
+ 'The other half of the same tube supplies the gain. The inverter half splits the signal and adds almost none: Robinette puts its gain at just under 2. On the drawing it has 56 kΩ plate and cathode loads.',
+ 'It’s never just one stage','When you’re playing hard, the input stage, the gain stage, the inverter, the output pair and the speaker can all be adding distortion at once.',
+ 'The topology is from Fender’s drawing. The marker is on V2. No clipping measurements were made.',true),
+ card('power-tubes','Cathode-biased 6V6 pair','rear','Cathode bias and no feedback loop.',
+ 'The two 6V6s share one cathode-bias resistor, and the 5E3 drawing shows no feedback loop from the speaker output back to an earlier stage. Those are two big differences from the blackface Deluxe Reverb, which also runs two 6V6s.',
  [['Output pair','Two 6V6GTs'],['Shared bias network','250 Ω / 5 W · 25 µF bypass']],
- 'Listen to how attack, compression and cleanup change with your picking and guitar volume. Those are whole-amp results; cathode bias alone does not guarantee a particular amount of softness.',
- 'Cathode voltage can move as the output pair’s average current changes under sustained drive. Removing global output feedback also changes how the power stage responds to distortion and the speaker load while it has headroom.',
- '“No feedback” needs a qualifier','There is no global speaker-output loop on this drawing. That does not mean there is no local feedback anywhere in the circuit, or that the amp is automatically operating in class A.',
- 'The marker identifies one of the paired output tubes. Bias voltages, output power and operating class were not measured.',true),
- card('rectifier','5Y3 power supply','rear','Some of the give comes from the supply.',
- 'The 5Y3GT rectifier is part of a supply that can drop and recover under a heavy playing load. That can contribute to the feeling of the amp giving way, then coming back.',
+ 'Players usually describe limited clean headroom at band volume, with the breakup arriving early and building gradually. Plenty of people want that for recording and small rooms. On a loud stage it can be a limit.',
+ 'The shared resistor is 250 Ω, 5 watts, with a 25 µF bypass. As the pair draws more current under sustained drive, the cathode voltage moves. With no global feedback, nothing is correcting the output stage’s distortion.',
+ 'What no feedback doesn’t mean','It means no loop from the speaker output on this drawing. It doesn’t mean there’s no local feedback anywhere, and it doesn’t make the amp class A.',
+ 'The marker is on one of the two output tubes. Bias voltage, output power and operating class weren’t measured.',true),
+ card('rectifier','5Y3 power supply','rear','Some of the give comes from the 5Y3.',
+ 'The 5Y3GT is one of the softer rectifiers Fender used. Hit the amp hard and the supply voltage can dip and recover, which players describe as sag or a swell after the attack.',
  [['Rectifier','5Y3GT'],['Filter drawing','Three 16 µF capacitors']],
- 'Compare a light touch with a sustained, hard attack when the amp is working. At quiet settings, the controls and speaker may explain more of what you hear than supply sag.',
- 'Rectifier voltage drop, transformer resistance, filter storage and current demand all affect the response. Not every bit of compression is supply sag, and not every tube-rectified amp has the same feel.',
- 'A tube label is not a voltage measurement','The drawing specifies the tube but does not give a measured supply voltage. We have not assigned one or measured the recovery time of the reference amp.',
- 'Socket order follows Fender’s layout. Transformer dimensions and all internal spacing remain estimates.',true),
- card('output-transformer','Output transformer & bass','rear','Big bass asks more of a small amp.',
- 'The output transformer couples the 6V6 pair to the speaker. How the whole amp handles bass at high level depends on that transformer, the coupling network, the supply and the speaker together.',
+ 'Compare a light touch with a hard, sustained attack when the amp’s working. At quiet settings the controls and the speaker probably explain more of what you hear than sag does.',
+ 'The drawing shows three 16 µF filter caps after the rectifier. Rectifier drop, transformer resistance, filtering and current draw all shape the response, so not every bit of compression is sag.',
+ 'People swap rectifiers','Some owners try a GZ34. It drops less voltage than a 5Y3, so the supply runs higher. Reports on the result vary, and the 5Y3 is the only rectifier on Fender’s drawing.',
+ 'Socket order follows Fender’s layout. The drawing gives no supply voltages and we haven’t measured any. Transformer size and internal spacing are estimates.',true),
+ card('output-transformer','Output transformer & bass','rear','Why the low end loosens up when it’s loud.',
+ 'At high settings, owners often describe the low end loosening and the whole sound compressing. Several stages are involved: the output transformer, the coupling caps, the supply and the speaker.',
  [['Coupling capacitors','0.1 µF at the output-tube grids'],['Unmeasured here','Transformer bandwidth and saturation']],
- 'A bass-heavy boost can make the low strings feel less defined when the amp is pushed. Compare with less bass before deciding that the speaker alone is the problem.',
- 'Low frequencies can consume headroom through several stages. The drawing’s relatively large coupling capacitors are relevant, but do not prove that a particular transformer saturates at a particular knob setting.',
- 'What the drawing does not establish','It does not state the output transformer’s primary impedance or a frequency-response curve. Neither is inferred from the size of our rendered block.',
- 'The block is a conceptual construction marker. Its mounting location, dimensions and windings have not been authenticated against the 1957 specimen.'),
- card('speakers','Jensen speaker & surviving cone','rear','The badge does not tell you what cone you are hearing.',
- 'The 1957 reference is listed with a Jensen P12R that has been reconed. That matters: the moving parts can change while the original frame and date code stay in place.',
+ 'A bass-heavy boost makes it worse on the low strings. Before you blame the speaker, back off the bass with Tone, your channel choice or the guitar’s controls, and listen again.',
+ 'The drawing has 0.1 µF coupling caps feeding the output-tube grids. Those are big enough to pass a lot of low end to stages that can’t handle it cleanly at volume. Robinette also points to the transformer saturating. That part is his analysis.',
+ 'What Fender’s drawing leaves out','It doesn’t give the output transformer’s primary impedance or any response curve, so we don’t state either.',
+ 'The block in the model is a rough marker. Its location, size and windings haven’t been checked against the 1957 amp.'),
+ card('speakers','Jensen speaker & surviving cone','rear','The label doesn’t tell you what cone you’re hearing.',
+ 'Our 1957 reference amp is listed with a Jensen P12R that’s been reconed. The frame and date code can stay the same while the parts that move get replaced.',
  [['Reference speaker','12-inch Jensen P12R · reconed'],['Dealer-listed code','220633 · week 33, 1956'],['Drawing load','One nominal 8 Ω speaker']],
- 'Before describing a recording as the sound of an original P12R, ask about the cone. Sensitivity, breakup and compression can change with a recone or replacement.',
- 'The speaker is a major part of the amp’s voice, alongside the open cabinet and circuit. The reference’s speaker date predates the amp’s February 1957 tube-chart code; it is a specimen record, not a universal production specification.',
- 'Keep the load in the comparison','A different speaker can change the sound while keeping the same nominal impedance. Changing the load also changes what the output stage sees through the transformer. We are not treating the extra jack as permission for any extension-cabinet combination.',
- 'The model is an estimated Jensen-family construction. No original cone, vintage wattage, measured response or sensitivity is claimed.')
+ 'Before you call a recording the sound of an original P12R, ask about the cone. A recone or a replacement can change sensitivity, breakup and compression.',
+ 'The dealer lists the speaker code as 220633, which is week 33 of 1956, earlier than the amp’s February 1957 tube-chart code. That’s a record of one amp, not a production spec.',
